    "round-trip judge left implication source_implies_translation unclear"
  ],
  "resolved_items": [
    {
      "item": "direct judge requested adjudication",
      "resolution": "Independent examination confirms the transport formula, scalar specialization, hyperbolicity, and nonvacuity. The unresolved issue is correspondence with the source's unspecified nonsmooth solution domain, rather than a demonstrated false Lean statement.",
      "primary_evidence": "Attached printed pages 1 and 3–5; target type; definitions D001–D010. The target separates differentiability and integrability into independent conditional branches."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge requested adjudication",
      "resolution": "The blind translation accurately reconstructs the target's mathematical content. Its observations about missing independent existence syntax do not establish missing solutions: every profile explicitly constructs a field satisfying the transport premise.",
      "primary_evidence": "D004 states q(x+c*t,t)=q(x,0). For any g, q(x,t)=g(x-c*t) satisfies this identity and q(x,0)=g(x), as in printed page 1, equation (1.3)."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge classification is undetermined",
      "resolution": "Retain undetermined because no exact admissibility convention for the full source family is established. Neither equivalence nor definite weakening follows merely from the explicit Lean regularity premises.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 1 says 'for any function'; printed pages 4–5 distinguish classical and discontinuous solutions without specifying an admissible function space. The target requires differentiability or interval integrability to obtain an analytic conclusion."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge classification is undetermined",
      "resolution": "Retain undetermined independently of the direct judgment. The reconstructed proposition and actual target have the same unresolved correspondence with the informal source, so there is no translation discrepancy that resolves classification.",
      "primary_evidence": "Blind target LocalDef001–LocalDef010 correspond to direct D001–D010; both target types contain the same two conditional branches and the same rectangle balance."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left dependency_coverage item D003 unclear",
      "resolution": "The declaration's meaning is resolved: it requires spatial integrability, temporal flux integrability, and exact oriented rectangle balance for all endpoints and times. It does not assert a classical derivative of interval mass at every time. Equivalence with the source's nonsmooth use of (1.10) remains unresolved.",
      "primary_evidence": "D003 explicitly contains three conjuncts and no HasDerivAt assertion. D087 defines oriented integration. Printed page 4 displays a time derivative in (1.10); page 5 says integral conservation continues for discontinuities."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left dependency_coverage item D021 unclear",
      "resolution": "The declaration's meaning is resolved at the supplied external frontier: integrability on both (a,b] and (b,a]. Quantifying all real a,b requires integrability on every bounded interval, not finite total mass over the real line. Its status as the source's exact admissibility condition remains unresolved.",
      "primary_evidence": "D021 explicitly expands to IntegrableOn on Set.Ioc a b and Set.Ioc b a. The target universally quantifies a,b. Printed pages 1 and 4 do not specify Lebesgue local integrability as the profile class."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left semantic_checklist item C04 unclear",
      "resolution": "The classical premise is appropriate and loses no globally classical translated profiles under D010: the required spatial derivative at time zero entails differentiability of the initial profile. The integral premise is appropriate for finite Lebesgue mass balances, but identifying that class with the entire source claim remains uncertain.",
      "primary_evidence": "D001 and D010 require both slice derivatives at every x,t, including t=0; D015 requires differentiability everywhere. Printed Section 1.1.2 qualifies classical equations by smoothness but does not define the full generalized domain."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left semantic_checklist item C05 unclear",
      "resolution": "Hyperbolicity, scalar reduction, translation, unchanged shape, and linear flux are represented. The transport premise does not omit the explicit solution-family construction. Completeness outside the two analytic domains, and recovery of (1.10) at exceptional times, remain unsettled.",
      "primary_evidence": "D002, D005–D010 represent hyperbolicity and scalar reduction. Substitution in D004 recovers (1.3). D003 receives flux state↦speed*state, matching printed page 4."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left semantic_checklist item C07 unclear",
      "resolution": "Exactness and signs pass. Rectangle conservation and pointwise time-differentiated mass conservation are not unconditionally equivalent for discontinuous profiles. This is a substantive solution-concept distinction, not an equality-direction error.",
      "primary_evidence": "For c=1 and g(z)=1 when z>0 and 0 otherwise, q(x,t)=g(x-t) has mass in [0,1] equal to 1 for t≤0, 1-t for 0<t<1, and 0 for t≥1. Rectangle balance holds, but the mass derivative fails at t=0 and t=1. Compare D003 with printed equation (1.10)."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left semantic_checklist item C10 unclear",
      "resolution": "There is no change of scalar field, dimension, signed velocity, or real-time domain. The branch premises make an explicit analytic-domain choice. That choice cannot be called genuine strengthening without first establishing that the full source applicability is preserved.",
      "primary_evidence": "The elaborated target uses Real throughout and Fin 1 for the matrix. Printed page 1 inherits R×R and m=1. The only additional analytic conditions occur as the two branch antecedents."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left semantic_checklist item N06 unclear",
      "resolution": "Existence of candidates is preserved by explicit construction, and neither a PDE converse nor uniqueness is asserted. The remaining issue is which candidates the source recognizes as generalized solutions.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 1 introduces (1.3) as an admitted family. The target's Iff compares D009 with D010 under D005 and D008; it does not compare either PDE predicate with D004."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C04 unclear",
      "resolution": "The translation correctly treats the differentiability and integrability premises separately. It does not require their conjunction. The differentiable branch matches classical translated solutions; the source's complete nonsmooth admissibility remains unspecified.",
      "primary_evidence": "The target has IsUniformAdvection q speed → ((Differentiable ... → ...) ∧ ((∀ a b, IntervalIntegrable ...) → ...)). Printed pages 4–5 explicitly distinguish classical and discontinuous interpretations."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C05 unclear",
      "resolution": "No omission is established by the lack of a separately quantified initial-profile witness or propagation conclusion: D004 parametrizes exactly the translated family. However, profiles outside the analytic premises receive no solution conclusion, and the source does not determine whether such profiles belong to its intended domain.",
      "primary_evidence": "D004 yields q(y,t)=q(y-c*t,0). A profile g(z)=1/|z| for z≠0 and g(0)=0 constructs a transported field but meets neither regularity premise under ordinary Lebesgue semantics. Printed page 1 does not explicitly address such singular profiles."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C06 unclear",
      "resolution": "The supplied declarations resolve the local operators, scalar actions, derivative-existence requirements, singleton matrix multiplication, and integral orientation. Standard external definitions remain a declared trust boundary. In particular, D040's inferInstance body does not independently expose the measure construction; no concrete measure mismatch is established.",
      "primary_evidence": "D009 and D010 contain genuine HasDerivAt witnesses; D103 uses the full neighborhood filter. D064 is the row dot product. D051 and D082 supply multiplication-based self-actions. D022 projects the measure, D040 displays inferInstance, and D087 supplies oriented integration."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C10 unclear",
      "resolution": "The blind reconstruction introduces no additional specialization beyond the actual target. Its regularity-domain choice remains a source-correspondence uncertainty and cannot justify faithful-stronger.",
      "primary_evidence": "The direct and anonymized explicit target types both specialize the generic solution predicates to Real and quantify all real speeds, points, and endpoints. Their regularity antecedents coincide."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item N06 unclear",
      "resolution": "The reconstructed family is nonvacuous and does not introduce uniqueness. A nonconstant polynomial profile activates both branches; a step profile activates the integral branch alone. Uncertainty concerns the intended full solution class, not satisfiability.",
      "primary_evidence": "Using q(x,t)=(x-c*t)^2 satisfies D004 and both regularity premises under ordinary real calculus. Using the translated step profile satisfies D004 and local integrability but fails global differentiability. D003 explicitly requires integrability."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left implication lean_implies_source unclear",
      "resolution": "The implication is established for the classical translated family and the time-integrated locally integrable family, but remains unclear for the entire informal source claim. The rectangle conclusion cannot supply an everywhere classical mass derivative for jumps.",
      "primary_evidence": "Instantiate the universal q binder with g(x-c*t), then apply D001 or D003 under the respective premise. Printed equation (1.3) leaves g unrestricted, and equation (1.10) retains derivative notation."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left implication source_implies_lean unclear",
      "resolution": "The algebraic and classical components are supported. The integral branch is mathematically derivable from the translation identity using ordinary Lebesgue change of variables and interval additivity. This derivability does not establish that its precise domain and solution concept are the source's selected meaning; that semantic implication remains unclear.",
      "primary_evidence": "D004 gives q(x,t)=g(x-c*t). For c≠0 affine substitution gives spatial and temporal trace integrability from D021; oriented interval additivity gives D003. For c=0 the field is stationary and the flux is zero. Printed pages 4–5 do not state this exact function-space theorem."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left implication translation_implies_source unclear",
      "resolution": "The reconstructed statement has the same partial implications and the same unresolved full-source scope as the Lean target. Its correct translation does not settle the source ambiguity.",
      "primary_evidence": "The blind translation reproduces D004's identity and all three conjuncts of D003, including every endpoint and time quantifier. The source's unrestricted profile wording and derivative-of-mass notation are visible on printed pages 1 and 4."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left implication source_implies_translation unclear",
      "resolution": "The reconstructed theorem is a coherent mathematical consequence of translation under its explicit analytic conventions. Its status as an equivalent rendering of the source remains unclear because those conventions are not fixed by the supplied source context.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed (1.2)–(1.3) support the chain-rule calculation; printed (1.10) fixes the flux orientation. The blind translation chooses local interval integrability and integrated time balance, whereas printed Section 1.1.2 supplies no exact admissibility or exceptional-time convention."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "lean_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "unclear",
      "reasoning": "Hyperbolicity and scalar specialization agree. Every differentiable profile constructs a q satisfying D004 and hence the classical PDE; every locally integrable profile similarly obtains rectangle conservation with the correct flux. These implications are nonvacuous and preserve translation. However, the supplied source does not specify whether these analytic domains exhaust 'any function', or how its derivative-of-mass equation is interpreted at exceptional times for discontinuous profiles. An implication to the entire selected source claim is therefore not established."
    },
    "source_implies_lean": {
      "verdict": "unclear",
      "reasoning": "The source supports the algebraic and classical assertions, and the rectangle theorem follows mathematically from the translated-profile identity under standard Lebesgue integration using affine substitution and interval additivity, including zero and negative velocities. Nevertheless, mathematical validity under these explicit conventions does not determine whether they preserve the source's exact generalized-solution meaning. The supplied context leaves that meaning unspecified, so full semantic implication remains unclear."
    }
  },
  "classification": "undetermined",
  "accepted": false,
  "remaining_uncertainties": [
    "The source does not identify the admissible function class intended by 'for any function' in the translated-profile claim.",
    "The source does not specify whether equation (1.10), for nonsmooth solutions, means an integrated balance, an almost-everywhere derivative identity, or a derivative identity subject to further qualifications.",
    "The supplied external frontier leaves the construction of Real.measureSpace unexpanded. Conventional Lebesgue semantics support the analysis above, but its construction was not independently verified from the displayed inferInstance body."
  ],
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "unresolved-source-admissibility",
      "evidence": "Printed page 1 gives unrestricted profile wording, while the target supplies analytic conclusions only under differentiability or integrability on every bounded interval. Printed pages 4–5 qualify classical interpretation but do not define the complete generalized domain.",
      "impact": "Full applicability cannot be certified. A definite weaker classification would also require an unsupported choice of a broader source domain."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "unresolved-conservation-interpretation",
      "evidence": "D003 asserts time-integrated rectangle balance. Printed (1.10) displays a time derivative of interval mass. A translated step has rectangle balance but interval mass with corners when the jump crosses an endpoint.",
      "impact": "Unconditional equivalence with an everywhere classical reading is false. Acceptance requires a source-supported interpretation of the nonsmooth conservation statement that the supplied pages do not make precise."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "transport-parametrization-and-nonvacuity",
      "evidence": "D004 is equivalent to q(x,t)=q(x-c*t,0). Every initial profile supplies such a q. Polynomial and step profiles provide substantive branch instances; Fin 1 is inhabited, and D002 requires a basis.",
      "impact": "The target does not assume its PDE conclusion, omit the family through missing existential syntax, or rely on an empty dimension or impossible premises."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "dependency-reuse-rechecked",
      "evidence": "The complete inventory supplies D009's derivative-vector body, D011's continuous pointwise scalar-action type, D103's derivative filter, and the relevant matrix, product-topology, and arithmetic definitions. Their current singleton specialization agrees with the reused interpretations.",
      "impact": "No disputed scalar-system mismatch is supported. Reuse provenance was not treated as evidence of task-specific source correspondence."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "category": "external-frontier-limitation",
      "evidence": "D040 displays only inferInstance; the blind translation explicitly adopts conventional real-volume semantics.",
      "impact": "This is an evidence limitation, not a demonstrated alternative measure or operator error. It does not resolve the independent source ambiguities."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The decision follows independent inspection of the attached primary-source renderings and supplied proof-free declaration evidence, not agreement between judges. The target faithfully preserves the identifiable algebraic structure and gives meaningful classical and integral transport statements. Its precise analytic formulation is mathematically coherent, but the supplied source does not settle the full admissibility and nonsmooth conservation conventions needed for unconditional semantic equivalence. Additional regularity premises cannot be credited as genuine strength, and no definite broader intended source domain has been established to justify rejection as weaker. Both full implications therefore remain unclear, consistently yielding undetermined and accepted=false. No tools were used, and no source or rendering hashes were independently recomputed."
}
